#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n![](images/55/55c749dccc54c252b9120f148e383f395c09fb4cd4bec0e1cce8ed441ecb6199.svg)\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n![](images/21/2181b471f34906eeaea7889abbba37c797a2f9624cee455b43b00fc048247cd1.svg)\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121036 :tutorial/content "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/dd/ddef437718d3a11811da02a17e2207d75e2d1852eeada67ab494af45b2a655e2.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n![](images/1f/1f92378ff1d1409f0928caa4b3ce98861784d0f2aa51dd1eda30e8877a0134ff.svg)\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3194 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span"}]}
